1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,718
Good morning, everyone.
Today, we're looking at

2
00:00:02,742 --> 00:00:05,372
the layout of a typical
Roman public bathhouse.

3
00:00:05,784 --> 00:00:08,176
I've put the main floor
plan up on the screen,

4
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:10,807
and I'll talk you through
each labeled area from A

5
00:00:10,808 --> 00:00:13,667
through to J, so please
follow along on your handout.

6
00:00:13,691 --> 00:00:17,824
Let's start at the entrance, on the west
side of the building. Visitors would first

7
00:00:17,848 --> 00:00:20,888
pass through the changing room,
which is labeled A on your plan.

8
00:00:20,889 --> 00:00:24,592
This is where bathers removed their
clothes and left them with an attendant,

9
00:00:24,593 --> 00:00:27,857
or occasionally lost them
to opportunistic thieves.

10
00:00:27,881 --> 00:00:30,900
From the changing room,
bathers moved east into

11
00:00:30,901 --> 00:00:34,842
the cold room, marked as B, a
space kept deliberately unheated.

12
00:00:34,866 --> 00:00:37,272
Next along the route
is the warm room,

13
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:40,042
labeled C, a transitional
space that allowed the

14
00:00:40,066 --> 00:00:42,567
body to adjust gradually
in temperature before

15
00:00:42,568 --> 00:00:45,042
entering the truly hot
areas of the building.

16
00:00:45,066 --> 00:00:47,892
Beyond that, at the eastern
end, sits the hot room,

17
00:00:48,004 --> 00:00:50,790
marked D on the plan,
the hottest space in the

18
00:00:50,814 --> 00:00:53,607
entire bathhouse. That
heat, of course, had to

19
00:00:53,608 --> 00:00:56,451
come from somewhere,
and that's the furnace, labeled

20
00:00:56,475 --> 00:00:59,167
E, located beneath the
hot room, so that its heat

21
00:00:59,168 --> 00:01:01,664
rose straight into the
hottest space without smoke

22
00:01:01,688 --> 00:01:03,946
entering the bathing
rooms. Going back to the

23
00:01:03,947 --> 00:01:06,553
changing room, to the
north of it we find the

24
00:01:06,577 --> 00:01:09,547
exercise yard, marked F,
an open area where bathers

25
00:01:09,548 --> 00:01:12,618
would exercise, wrestle, or
play ballgames before bathing.

26
00:01:12,642 --> 00:01:14,627
And to the south of
the changing room

27
00:01:14,628 --> 00:01:17,949
is the garden, labeled G, a
planted, shaded space where

28
00:01:17,973 --> 00:01:21,859
visitors could relax and socialize
away from the heat and noise.

29
00:01:21,860 --> 00:01:24,624
Back inside, close to
the cold room, you'll

30
00:01:24,648 --> 00:01:27,912
find the cold plunge pool,
marked H, a deep pool of

31
00:01:27,999 --> 00:01:30,613
cold water bathers would
immerse themselves in as a

32
00:01:30,637 --> 00:01:33,207
bracing finish to their
visit. Next to that is the

33
00:01:33,208 --> 00:01:36,100
massage room, labeled 1,
where bathers could be oiled

34
00:01:36,124 --> 00:01:38,872
and massaged by attendants,
a standard part of the

35
00:01:38,884 --> 00:01:41,781
full bathing experience.
And finally, tucked away near

36
00:01:41,805 --> 00:01:44,552
the entrance for practical
reasons, is the latrine,

37
00:01:44,739 --> 00:01:47,407
marked J on your plan,
a communal toilet facility,

38
00:01:47,431 --> 00:01:50,580
often built with quite
sophisticated plumbing for its time.

39
00:01:50,581 --> 00:01:52,848
Now let's move to the
second diagram, which

40
00:01:52,872 --> 00:01:55,467
shows the underfloor
heating system, known as the

41
00:01:55,468 --> 00:01:58,448
hypocost, that made all
this hot bathing possible.

42
00:01:58,472 --> 00:02:01,472
Again, I'll go through
the labels A to J in order.

43
00:02:01,859 --> 00:02:04,601
At the start of the system
is the furnace mouth,

44
00:02:04,625 --> 00:02:07,547
labeled A, where slaves
would continually feed fuel

45
00:02:07,548 --> 00:02:09,915
to keep the fire burning
throughout the day.

46
00:02:09,939 --> 00:02:12,907
The heat and hot air generated
there passed beneath the

47
00:02:12,908 --> 00:02:15,905
floor, which was supported
on brick pillars, marked

48
00:02:15,929 --> 00:02:18,592
B. These held the floor
above a hollow space,

49
00:02:18,800 --> 00:02:21,418
allowing hot air to
circulate freely underneath.

50
00:02:21,442 --> 00:02:24,287
Resting on top of those
pillars was the raised floor

51
00:02:24,288 --> 00:02:27,186
itself, labeled C, which
bathers actually walked

52
00:02:27,210 --> 00:02:30,367
on, and which grew
uncomfortably hot in the hot room

53
00:02:30,368 --> 00:02:33,449
directly above the furnace.
Heat didn't only travel

54
00:02:33,473 --> 00:02:36,447
under the floor. It also
rose through hollow wall

55
00:02:36,448 --> 00:02:39,319
tiles, marked D, allowing
warmth to spread up the

56
00:02:39,343 --> 00:02:43,165
walls and heat the room far more
evenly than floor heating alone.

57
00:02:43,166 --> 00:02:45,539
Any smoke and
excess hot air eventually

58
00:02:45,563 --> 00:02:48,532
had to escape, and it did
so through the chimney,

59
00:02:48,570 --> 00:02:51,990
labeled E, which drew air
up and out of the building.

60
00:02:52,014 --> 00:02:55,252
Below the furnace itself,
ash naturally collected,

61
00:02:55,340 --> 00:02:57,796
and this was managed
by the ash pit, marked F,

62
00:02:57,820 --> 00:03:00,607
which needed to be cleared
out regularly to keep the

63
00:03:00,608 --> 00:03:03,738
furnace burning efficiently.
Nearby, the fuel store,

64
00:03:03,762 --> 00:03:06,852
labeled G, held the wood
needed to keep the furnace

65
00:03:07,090 --> 00:03:10,362
supplied, positioned close
enough for convenient access

66
00:03:10,386 --> 00:03:13,492
but far enough to avoid
becoming a fire risk itself.

67
00:03:13,530 --> 00:03:17,720
Workers moved between the furnace and the
storage areas along the service passage,

68
00:03:17,770 --> 00:03:20,752
marked H, a narrow corridor
kept separate from the

69
00:03:20,776 --> 00:03:23,647
public bathing areas. To
help control conditions

70
00:03:23,648 --> 00:03:26,038
inside the hot room,
the system included a

71
00:03:26,062 --> 00:03:28,927
temperature vent, labeled
I, which could be opened

72
00:03:28,928 --> 00:03:32,396
or closed to regulate how much
heat reached the room above.

73
00:03:32,420 --> 00:03:34,692
And finally, for
maintenance purposes,

74
00:03:34,780 --> 00:03:37,727
engineers built in an
inspection hatch, marked J,

75
00:03:37,751 --> 00:03:41,007
allowing workers to check
and clean the hypocost space

76
00:03:41,008 --> 00:03:44,015
without having to dig up the
floor above. That completes

77
00:03:44,039 --> 00:03:47,007
our tour of the bathhouse.
Next week, we'll look at how

78
00:03:47,008 --> 00:03:50,120
how these buildings function
socially within Roman towns.

